          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which chapter holds high blood pressure, and what does that chapter deliberately exclude?",
          "answer": "**Chapter:** Essential (primary) hypertension is code I10 in ICD-10-CM. It sits in Chapter 9, Diseases of the circulatory system (I00-I99), in the hypertensive-disease block I10 to I16. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*; *ICD-10-CM Chapter 9*.)\n\n**What the chapter sends elsewhere:** The retrieved passages name these exclusions:\n\n- **Transient ischemic attack (TIA)** is classified with the nervous system, not with the other cerebrovascular diseases.\n- **Congenital malformations of the heart and great vessels** belong to the congenital chapter, even in adults.\n- **Circulatory disorders during pregnancy or childbirth** are classified in the obstetric chapter.\n- **An elevated blood-pressure reading** in someone with no diagnosis of hypertension is a symptom or finding, coded in Chapter 18 (R00-R09).\n- **Obstructive sleep apnea** is classified with the nervous system, not with the respiratory chapter. This is from the respiratory chapter passage.\n\nPulmonary hypertension is not excluded. It is a circulatory condition, at I26 to I28.\n\n**The convention:** The passages describe where codes live, but they do not name the formal rule that governs these exclusions (such as \"Excludes1\" or \"Excludes2\" notes). I cannot confirm that rule from this material, so please check the official ICD-10-CM tabular list or a coding reference for the exact wording.\n\n**Other points from the passages:**\n- I10 is complete at three characters. It takes no further digits, and \"malignant hypertension\" has no separate code in this version.\n- Where hypertension is linked to heart or kidney disease, a combination code is used instead of I10. Those codes are not covered in detail in the retrieved material.\n\nThis is general coding information, not a diagnosis. If you have questions about your own blood pressure, a clinician is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
